{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass at that time.</b> This is not the time when Joseph was sold, but before his sale. Similarly: \"From there they set out for Gudgodah\" (Deuteronomy 10:7), \"At that time YHWH set apart the tribe of Levi\" (ibid. 10:8) — yet the tribe of Levi was chosen in the second year, and they set out for Gudgodah in the fortieth year; I will explain it in its place. And why did Scripture mention this episode here — it should have been after \"And the Midianites sold him,\" at the passage \"And Joseph was brought down to Egypt\"? To separate between the matter of Joseph and the matter of his master's wife, and the matter of his brothers. And I needed this explanation, because from the day Joseph was sold until the day our ancestors went down to Egypt there are only twenty-two years, yet Onan was born — he being the second of Judah's sons — and grew up until he had seed, and this cannot be less than twelve years; and furthermore, \"and the days multiplied,\" and Tamar conceived and gave birth to Perez, and he came to Egypt and already has two sons. And let not the matter of Bezalel trouble you, for when I reach his place I will explain it."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra cites Deuteronomy 10:7–8; the second citation reference 'ibid. 10:8' is implied by 'שם שם ח' (there, there 8), i.e., same chapter, verse 8."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:47:24.445521379Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}